Fillets: a rounding whose end section lies in a face at the vertex where it
ends, the blend running on into that face tangentially (mitcad#236): e.g. a
vault's end edge rounded with the radius of the block's rounded vertical
corners under it. The ball overflows the corners onto the end face, and
where the vault springs tangentially from the side walls its section is the
corner's own quarter circle. OCCT found no face at the end holding both end
points (one is the vertex, the other lies inside the end face, on its edge
with the corner), went on to its corner of several faces and failed there
("Failed to find edge").

- ChFi3d_EndSectionOnFace, tried in PerformOneCorner (not OnSame) where no
  face at the end is found otherwise: a face at the corner's vertex other
  than the blend's two faces that holds the whole end section (five points
  of it within ten times the approximation tolerance, its middle inside the
  face) is the face at the end. An end point on no arc gets the edge of that
  face it lies on that also bounds the blend's face on its side, with the
  transition of a contact entering (first end) or leaving (last end) that
  face across it; an end point at a vertex of that face is that vertex
  only: no arc is cut there, so that the faces beyond the blend keep their
  edges up to it.
- The cap is then the end section itself (ChFi3d_ComputeArete on the blend
  between the contacts' ends, its curve on the face at the end from the same
  3d curve), not the intersection of two surfaces that are tangent along
  it. Its orientation on the face at the end is taken from the arc on the
  face.
- The spine's edge at a vertex end point is cut at the vertex (as at a
  singular end, ChFi3d_FilDS): no cut of an arc separates it from the faces
  that keep it, and it went on to bound the blend's face and the face at the
  end.
- ChFi3d_Builder::PerformSetOfSurfOnElSpine: a walk whose blend reached
  the vertex at the spine's end with one contact, at a section before the
  guide's end, failed on the next step from that vertex and invalidated the
  stripe. The blend now ends there (as where the walk past the end stops
  outside a face), and the corner is built at the vertex. With the end
  section in the corner's face the walk stops there or a little past it
  depending on its steps.
